; A bare FIXED VALUE as the target of a COUNT-PERMITTING occurrence (`[* 5]`; equally `[+ 5]`,
; `[? 5]`, `[2*5 5]`, `[* true]`, `[* "v1"]`, `[* null]`) is REJECTED GRACEFULLY in the parse walk
; (`parse_group_type`), so the generator exits 1 with an actionable message rather than aborting.
; The refusal is what the shape actually means: a homogeneous array stores its elements in a
; `Vec<T>`, and a `Fixed` has no `T` — it exists only as an unstored member whose value the schema
; pins, so there is nothing to store per repetition. A rejection is recorded rather than the marker
; being dropped onto the record path, because a one-element record would make the generated decoder
; accept a single-element array for a `0..N` spec — a certified over-acceptance, which is worse
; than a refusal. Its table sibling is the same class: a fixed VALUE domain (`{ * uint => 5 }`, and
; the parenthesized `{ * (uint => 5) }`) has no per-row type either, and is rejected by the same
; guard family. The message names no false remedy — naming the constant as its own rule resolves to
; the same `Fixed` (and is itself rejected at the alias seam), so it offers only what really works,
; the EXACTLY-ONCE placement (`[5]`, tests/robustness/fixed_bool_member.cddl), which stays supported
; and is deliberately outside the guard — and it says plainly that widening to the constant's CDDL
; type is a different spec, not an equivalent one.
;
; A fixed KEY domain (`{ 1 => uint }`) is deliberately NOT this class: per RFC 8610 it is the same
; wire entry as the colon spelling, so it diverts to the record path and generates.
a = [* 5]
